ormalizing and in many ways  standardizing the production of this  protein source potential economies of  scale offer another way for insect  farming to challenge the inefficiencies  of traditional and environmentally  unsustainable animal agriculture so our  first harvest we're expecting to be mid  next year and then i'm really gonna be  busy yeah yeah yeah for sure make sure  you get all the sleep you need right now  our facility today that we're building  that's gonna come online next year  is situated on 12 acres of property now  according to the usda 12 acres is just  about enough land to raise approximately  three or four cows three or four cows  means you're producing maybe maybe you  know a few thousand kilograms a year of  meat from that 12 acre parcel of land  we are going to produce 12 million  kilograms a year of crickets from 12  acres  farming and eating insects at scale also  has the potential to reduce greenhouse  gas emissions from food production  crickets produce around half as much  carbon dioxide per kilogram compared  with chicken fun fact crickets don't  actually emit methane so their  greenhouse gas emissions which are  already low are even vanishingly smaller  as a result of that  but there's a very long way to go before  insects come close to challenging  livestock and other food industries  supply will need to increase  dramatically  part of why adoption hasn't been more  forthcoming is a lack of scalable supply  in order for you to be able to meet the  volume requirements of some of the  largest food companies in the world they  require  thousands upon thousands of metric tons  just to be able to launch in some  markets with one product i mean the  combined output of our entire industry  today doesn't even meet that  insects could crawl into many more foods  from protein bars to pizzas  whether insects become a staple of the  global diet may depend on many factors  from climate change  to  psychology the 1970s  eating raw fish was unthinkable in many  countries  but today sushi is found on supermarket  shelves across the world  there may yet be a change in the global  appetite for insects  [Music]  thanks very much for watching you can  read more about the future of food and  my technology quarterly by clicking the  link and please don't forget to  subscribe  you

